-------------------------------------------------------

		Vue JS
Things you NEED to know:
Used to build user interfaces adn single-page applications.
Focuses on the view layer (UI) while providing tools and
patterns for building scalable, maintainable front-end applications.

Vue.js is built around a few core ideas:
	- Reactive data binding
	- Component-based architecture
	- One-way data flow (props down, events up)
	- Efficient rendering through caching
	- Flexible composition with the Composition API

- Directives:
	v-bind (:) → bind data to attributes
	v-model → two-way binding (forms)
	v-if / v-else / v-show → conditional rendering
	v-for → loops
	v-on (@) → event listeners
	Example:
		<button @click="count++">Clicked {{ count }} times</button>
	- Its about data ownership

- Methods 
	- Do something when you tell them to
	- Use when need to trigger logic via an event)
	Example:
		methods: {						>		methods: {
		  greet() {		  				>		  methodName(param){
			console.log("Hello");		>		    action;
		  }}		  					>		  }}

- Computed
	- Calculate something automatically and cache it
	- Use when:
		- You're returning a value based on existing state
		- Deriving data from other data
		- Performance optimization
		- You want clean templates
	Example (filtering lists / formatting values / combining data):
		computed: {
		  filteredUsers() {
		    return this.users.filter(u => u.active);
		  }}

- Watch
	- React when something changes
	- NOT for returning values
	- Use when you need to:
		- call an API
		- perform async work
		- react to changes over time
	- Example
		- Search input → fetch API
		- Form changes → autosave
		- Route changes → reload data

- Props
	- Pass data
	- Passing data into a component
	- Props are how a parent gives data to a child
	- ! Props are READ-ONLY, do not modify them
	- Use props when:
		- Data comes from a parent
		- You want reusable components
		- You want a one-way data flow
	Example:
	[Parent]
		<user-card :name="username" />
	[Child]
		export default {
		  props: {
			name: String
		  }}
	[HTML]
		<p>{{ name }}</p>

- Emits
	- Send events
	- Sending events back up
	- Props go down, Emits go up
	- Use Emits when:
		- A child needs to notify the parent
		- You're handling user interaction
		- You want to keep components decoupled
	Example:
	[Parent]
		methods: {
		  handleUpdate(newName) {
			this.username = newName;
		  }}
	[HTML]
		<user-card @updateName="handleUpdate" />
	[Child]
		export default {
		  emits: ['updateName'],
		  methods: {
			changeName() {
			  this.$emit('updateName', 'John');
			}
		  }
		}
	[HTML]
		<button @click="changeName">Change Name</button>

- Slots
	- Passes structure (HTML)
	- Slots let a parent inject content into a child component
	- With slots, parents can control the content
	The basic idea:
	[Parent HTML]
		<card>
		  <h2>Hello</h2>
		  <p>This is inside the card</p>
		</card>
	[Child HTML]
		<div class="card">
		  <slot></slot>
		</div>

- V-model
	- Under the hood:
		- prop: modelValue
		- emit: update:modelValue
	- This is how two-way binging works between components
	- Two-way binding means data flows in both directions between your UI and your state
		- UI updates data
		- Data updates UI
	Example usage:
		<custom-input v-model="name" />

- Composition API (modern Vue)
	- Gives more control, better code organisation and makes large applications easier to manage
	- Key building blocks:
		- setup() → the entry point where everything happens
		- ref() → reactive values
			import { ref } from 'vue';
			const count = ref(0);
		- reactive() → reactive objects
			import { reactive } from 'vue';
			const user = reactive({
			  name: "John",
			  age: 25
			});
		- computed() → derived values
			import { computed } from 'vue';
			const doublee = computed (() => count.value * 2);
		- watch() → react to changes
			import { watch } from 'vue';
			watch(count, (newVal) => {
			  console.log(newVal);
			});
		- Return values → expose to template
			return { count, increment };

- Event Handling & Modifiers
	- v-on (@ shorthand) to listen to events
	[HTML]
		<button @click="increment">Click me</button>
	[JS]
		methods: {
		  increment() {
		    this.count++;
		  }}
	Passing arguments:
	[HTML]
		<button @click="add(5)">Add</button>
	[JS]
		methods: {
		  add(num) {
		    this.count += num;
		  }}
	- .prevent → Prevent default behavior
		<form @submit.prevent="submitForm">  |  Stops page reload on form submit
	- .stop → Stop event bubbling
		<button @click.stop="doSomething">  |  Prevents the event from propagating to parent elements
	- .once → Runs only once
		<button @click.once="init">  |  Useful for one-time actions
	- .self → Only trigger if event comes from the element itself
		<div @click.self="close">  |  Ignores clicks from child elements
	- .capture → Use capture phase
		<div @click.capture="handle">  |  Runs before normal phase (advanced use)

- Key Attribute (critical for performance)
	<li v-for="item in items" :key="item.id">

- Caching
	- Storing a result so you don't have to recompute it again
		computed: {
		  fullName() {
			return this.first + " " + this.last;
		  }}
	- Run this once
	- Store (cache) the result
	- Only re-run if first or last changes

	Cache vs Memory
	- Cache stores computed values/results
	- Memory stores data
	- Cache updates when dependencies change
	- Memory changes whenever you assign